# Events App — Agent Context

## What this is

A React Native (Expo) app for sharing events between people. No feeds — just a calendar of events shared person-to-person. Sharing triggers person-to-person notifications: push for recipients who have the app, SMS for those who don't.

Read `docs/events-technical-architecture.md` for the full architecture, data model, and flows. That document is the source of truth for how the app should behave.

## Stack

- **Frontend:** React Native, Expo SDK 54, Expo Router v6 (file-based routing), TypeScript
- **Backend:** Supabase — Postgres, Auth (phone OTP), Edge Functions (Deno), RLS
- **Key libraries:** react-native-calendars, libphonenumber-js, @react-native-async-storage/async-storage, expo-notifications

## Key architectural rules

- **Events are immutable.** Never UPDATE an events row. Edits create a new row (fork). The user's `user_events` row is updated to point to the new event_id.
- **Sharing is forwarding.** The `share_event` RPC delivers each recipient their own `user_events` copy at share time (contacts without accounts get theirs on sign-up via the `deliver_pending_shares` trigger). A share is a completed action — there is no unshare, and removing your copy never affects anyone else's calendar. `event_shares` is the share record (attribution, notifications, pending delivery), not the visibility mechanism.
- **Sharing is person-to-person.** Circles are a UI shortcut — at the data level, `event_shares` always reference individual `my_people` rows.
- **No forced onboarding.** Users land on the calendar immediately after sign-up; someone who was shared an event sees it right away. The walkthrough (`app/(app)/onboarding.tsx`) auto-shows at most once and only when the user has no events at all; it can be reopened anytime via the `?` button in the calendar header. Contacts permission is only requested when they tap Share or navigate to My People.
- **Dedup is server-side.** Use the `find_or_create_event` RPC to create events. Don't do insert-then-query dedup from the client.
- **Web is a first-class frontend.** The same Expo codebase runs in the browser via react-native-web and is hosted at **https://shared-events.pages.dev** (Cloudflare Pages; Wrangler project name `shared-events` — `events-app.pages.dev` was globally taken). The staging preview lives at **https://staging.shared-events.pages.dev** (Pages branch alias for `staging`). Web gaps are handled explicitly: manual "Add person" (name + phone) on the People screen (no contacts API on web), and HTML `date`/`time` inputs in the add/edit event forms (the native datetimepicker never opens on web). Never request browser notification permission — web users get SMS. Deploy with `npm run deploy:web` / `npm run deploy:staging` (see AGENTS.md).
- **RLS enforces access.** Every table has Row-Level Security. Users can only see their own data or data shared with them. Never use `USING (true)` on events.
- **Pull, not push.** The app fetches fresh data on screen focus. No real-time subscriptions. Notifications are person-triggered only (a share action calls `send-notification`) — never broadcasts or marketing pushes.
- **Delete means remove.** Deleting an event deletes only the caller's own `user_events` row (their `event_shares` records cascade). The app never deletes `events` rows; `cleanup-events` (cron, `CRON_SECRET` header) reclaims only snapshots with zero remaining owners — it must never delete `user_events`/`event_shares`.
- **Don't withhold to drive opens.** SMS messages include the event URL (plus store links for non-app users) so recipients can act without installing the app.
- **Themes are named moods, not OS light/dark.** Paper (default) and Evening are user-selected via the theme swatch button in the calendar header (previews the next theme's colors; cycles the registry) and persisted to AsyncStorage (`theme_preference`). Every color/type decision comes from a role token in `constants/Colors.ts` via `useTheme` — never hard-code hex values, and never read the OS color scheme. The accent is spent only on the selected day, event dots, the primary action, and the "From X" attribution; destructive is always red. `docs/events-design-language.md` is the source of truth. On web, `applyWebBrowserChrome` keeps the browser chrome (iOS Safari status bar / Dynamic Island tint, `theme-color`, `color-scheme`, `html`/`body` background) in sync with the active theme — react-native-web's `StatusBar` is a no-op.

